sentences.l:
And that, in his hand having taken, wide-ruling Agamemnon
drew toward himself, eager, like a lion, and out of the hand
wrenched it;

So he, on the spot having fallen, slept a bronze sleep,
pitiable, far from his wedded wife, helping the townsmen,
the wedded one, of whom no delight he saw, though much he gave;

These then he, as leaders of the Danaans, took, and then afterwards
the mass of men, as when the clouds the West Wind drives
of the bright South Wind, with a deep squall striking;

Then would ruin have been, and deeds past help have come to pass,
and now among the ships would have fallen, fleeing, the Achaeans,
if not to Tydeus’-son Diomedes had called Odysseus:
